schema: encyclopedia-entry
object:
  title: "Ontology vs Taxonomy: Which One Does Your Commons Need?"
  type: encyclopedia-entry
  page_type: comparison
  summary: >-
    A plain-language guide for teams building a knowledge commons: a taxonomy
    is a labelled filing cabinet (hierarchy of categories, preferred/alternate
    labels, broader/narrower/related relations) for browsing and finding
    things; an ontology adds typed classes, individuals, and named relationships
    that let software infer facts (e.g. flagging a missing report). The
    practical heuristic: taxonomy first, ontology only when a specific,
    recurring task needs machine reasoning over relationships. SKOS is offered
    as the bridge that keeps the door to an ontology open without wasted work.
  audience: small-team leads building a knowledge commons who are comfortable in docs/GitHub but not ontologists
  known_tensions:
    - "taxonomy-vs-ontology scope creep — promoting every useful tag to a structural type balloons the model (ontology sprawl)"
    - "classification (taxonomy) vs. machine-reasoning (ontology) are often conflated in practice"
  related_concepts:
    - taxonomy
    - ontology
    - SKOS
  related_resources:
    - SKOS (W3C standard)
    - Protégé (ontology editor)
    - Schema.org
    - Wikidata
  maturity: raw
  ai_assisted: true
  provenance:
    origin: src/content/docs/ontology-vs-taxonomy.md
    transformation: summarized
    authorship: ai-assisted

